WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.450
The king of Egypt does not thank the god who invented writing; he says,

2
00:00:04.450 --> 00:00:16.260
"This is a drug not for remembering but for forgetting." Twenty-four hundred years later we read that same sentence as people who cannot find their way without navigation and cannot recall a single phone number without a phone.

3
00:00:16.260 --> 00:00:21.830
So does the tool merely make our work easier, or is it quietly rewriting us as well?

4
00:00:30.030 --> 00:00:37.200
The story takes place in a city in lower Egypt, in an age when the gods were still assumed to speak with human beings.

5
00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:46.290
There was a god named Theuth; he was the one who discovered number and calculation, geometry, the map of the stars, even games played with dice.

6
00:00:46.290 --> 00:00:50.900
But there was one invention he boasted of above all the rest: letters.

7
00:00:50.900 --> 00:00:52.950
Writing.

8
00:00:52.950 --> 00:00:57.240
One day he comes before Thamus, the king of the land.

9
00:00:57.240 --> 00:01:03.610
He lays out all his inventions one by one, and for each of them the king offers either praise or criticism.

10
00:01:03.610 --> 00:01:07.740
When the turn comes to writing, Theuth grows excited.

11
00:01:07.740 --> 00:01:15.550
Look, he says, this discovery will make the Egyptians wiser and strengthen their memory; this is a remedy for both recollection and wisdom.

12
00:01:15.550 --> 00:01:23.840
The king looks at him for a long moment, and gives this answer: the person who invents a thing is the worst possible judge of its usefulness.

13
00:01:23.840 --> 00:01:29.250
Because you are the father of letters, you attribute to writing a power it does not have.

14
00:01:29.250 --> 00:01:33.700
Writing will not strengthen memory; it will produce forgetfulness.

15
00:01:33.700 --> 00:01:39.270
People will no longer remember from within themselves — they will trust to marks outside them.

16
00:01:39.270 --> 00:01:43.880
What you are giving them is not the remedy of memory, but of reminding.

17
00:01:43.880 --> 00:01:50.410
Your students will read a great deal and yet learn nothing; they will not be wise, they will merely seem wise.

18
00:01:50.410 --> 00:02:00.460
The one who passes this conversation on to us is Socrates, sitting outside the city walls of Athens, in the shade of a plane tree, his feet in the cool water of a stream.

19
00:02:00.460 --> 00:02:06.350
It is an Egyptian tale he tells to his young companion in the dialogue of Plato's known as the Phaedrus.

20
00:02:06.350 --> 00:02:16.720
And here, buried inside the story, stands one of the most elegant ironies in human history: the only reason we know of this conversation today is that Plato wrote it down.

21
00:02:16.720 --> 00:02:21.970
The most powerful objection ever raised against writing has reached us in writing.

22
00:02:21.970 --> 00:02:27.220
Socrates wrote nothing; we know him only because he had a student who did.

23
00:02:27.220 --> 00:02:31.510
This irony is not a contradiction but a diagnosis.

24
00:02:31.510 --> 00:02:35.160
Because the word Theuth uses is itself a trap.

25
00:02:35.160 --> 00:02:41.370
In Greek he says "pharmakon" — a word that means both remedy and poison.

26
00:02:41.370 --> 00:02:46.460
The same word, depending on the dose, says two things at once.

27
00:02:46.460 --> 00:02:57.310
The French thinker Jacques Derrida, in a famous text written in nineteen sixty-eight, dwells on precisely this word: in describing writing, Plato seems, without quite realizing it,

28
00:02:57.310 --> 00:03:00.960
to have stated the common law of all technologies.

29
00:03:00.960 --> 00:03:03.810
No tool brings benefit alone.

30
00:03:03.810 --> 00:03:08.260
What it brings and what it takes away are two faces of the same mechanism.

31
00:03:08.260 --> 00:03:13.030
There's an easy misunderstanding here; let's clear it up at the outset.

32
00:03:13.030 --> 00:03:20.520
What Thamus is saying is not "writing is bad." The king is not banning writing, he is pointing to an accounting error.

33
00:03:20.520 --> 00:03:25.770
Theuth counts what his invention will win us, but not what it will cost us.

34
00:03:25.770 --> 00:03:28.140
And cost us it did.

35
00:03:28.140 --> 00:03:40.110
In oral cultures there were people who carried epics thousands of lines long entirely by heart; the poems of Homer lived for centuries without ever being written down, circulating only among trained memories.

36
00:03:40.110 --> 00:03:43.760
That muscle of memory slackened with the coming of writing.

37
00:03:43.760 --> 00:03:53.010
But that same writing made possible thought too large for any single head to hold; it set mathematics, law, and history on ground solid enough to build upon.

38
00:03:53.010 --> 00:03:59.380
What was lost was a real loss, what was gained was a real gain, and the two were not exchangeable.

39
00:03:59.380 --> 00:04:03.190
Here is the heart of the matter: this trade has no contract.

40
00:04:03.190 --> 00:04:15.640
Nobody ever sat down and signed a document saying, "I hereby surrender part of my memory in return for the reach of my thought." The change happened through use, unnoticed, completed within a few generations.

41
00:04:15.640 --> 00:04:21.050
And by the time it was complete, there was no one left who remembered the earlier state.

42
00:04:21.050 --> 00:04:28.380
How the mind worked before writing is something we can only guess at today, indirectly, from anthropological traces.

43
00:04:28.380 --> 00:04:32.190
The loss took place without even announcing that it was a loss.

44
00:04:32.190 --> 00:04:37.440
This is the most insidious aspect of the relationship a tool builds with a human being.

45
00:04:37.440 --> 00:04:40.130
A tool extends a capacity.

46
00:04:40.130 --> 00:04:44.420
But at the same moment it lays off the organ that carried that capacity.

47
00:04:44.420 --> 00:04:48.550
And in the early days, the name for being laid off is relief.

48
00:04:48.550 --> 00:04:55.560
Atrophy and relief are, at the beginning, indistinguishable from one another; both give the same feeling of lightness.

49
00:04:55.560 --> 00:04:59.690
The difference only emerges when the tool is taken out of your hands.

50
00:04:59.690 --> 00:05:02.860
On that day, you see what you have left.

51
00:05:02.860 --> 00:05:10.190
Thamus's question is still waiting to be answered twenty-five hundred years later, because every new tool asks it again.

52
00:05:10.190 --> 00:05:21.840
And the question is not "is this tool good or bad?" The question is this: am I using this tool, or is it, without my noticing, turning me into a human being that suits it?

53
00:05:21.840 --> 00:05:28.370
To pursue that question, we first have to notice something strange: tools are invisible for as long as they work.

54
00:05:28.370 --> 00:05:38.100
Martin Heidegger, the German philosopher, explained this with a very simple example in his book Being and Time, published in nineteen twenty-seven: a hammer.

55
00:05:38.100 --> 00:05:42.870
When a carpenter is working with a hammer, he does not think about the hammer.

56
00:05:42.870 --> 00:05:47.960
He doesn't notice its weight, the grain of its handle, the coldness of the metal.

57
00:05:47.960 --> 00:05:56.250
His mind isn't even on the nail; it's on the cabinet as a whole, on the job he has to finish by evening, perhaps on something else entirely.

58
00:05:56.250 --> 00:05:59.740
The hammer has all but dissolved into his hand.

59
00:05:59.740 --> 00:06:03.230
Heidegger calls this readiness-to-hand.

60
00:06:03.230 --> 00:06:08.960
Precisely when a tool is working, it withdraws from consciousness; it becomes transparent.

61
00:06:08.960 --> 00:06:12.290
So when does the hammer become visible?

62
00:06:12.290 --> 00:06:14.980
When the handle cracks.

63
00:06:14.980 --> 00:06:17.350
When the head flies off.

64
00:06:17.350 --> 00:06:20.680
Or when you can't find it at the very moment you need it.

65
00:06:20.680 --> 00:06:28.970
Breakdown brings the tool suddenly before your eyes; what was until then an extension of your hand turns all at once into an object standing opposite you.

66
00:06:28.970 --> 00:06:35.340
In other words, the moment we truly see a tool is not the moment we use it, but the moment we can't.

67
00:06:35.340 --> 00:06:45.870
That strange emptiness you feel when your phone's battery dies is exactly this: something that has melted into your hand and gone invisible over the years becomes visible again by breaking.

68
00:06:45.870 --> 00:06:50.320
This transparency is a tool's greatest gift and its greatest danger.

69
00:06:50.320 --> 00:06:54.130
Because you cannot notice how something invisible is shaping you.

70
00:06:54.130 --> 00:07:01.940
Years later, in a lecture given in nineteen fifty-three and published the following year, Heidegger reckons with technology itself.

71
00:07:01.940 --> 00:07:04.950
His claim here is far more unsettling.

72
00:07:04.950 --> 00:07:11.000
Technology, he says, is not merely a matter of instruments; it is a way of seeing.

73
00:07:11.000 --> 00:07:14.810
We begin to look at the world through a particular eye.

74
00:07:14.810 --> 00:07:17.340
He takes the Rhine as his example.

75
00:07:17.340 --> 00:07:24.350
For centuries the Rhine was a river poets wrote about, a river with cities built on its banks and bridges thrown across it.

76
00:07:24.350 --> 00:07:33.760
Once a hydroelectric plant is built on it, the river is no longer a landscape upon which the plant stands; it becomes a source from which the plant is fed.

77
00:07:33.760 --> 00:07:37.890
The river is redefined as a reserve of pressurized water.

78
00:07:37.890 --> 00:07:44.420
Heidegger calls this standing-reserve: everything reduced to stock, piled up and waiting to be used.

79
00:07:44.420 --> 00:07:48.230
And this way of looking does not stop at the river.

80
00:07:48.230 --> 00:07:58.440
The eye that sees timber when it looks at a forest, ore when it looks at a mountain, yield when it looks at soil, sooner or later turns the same eye on human beings.

81
00:07:58.440 --> 00:08:02.250
Think about how natural the phrase human resources sounds.

82
00:08:02.250 --> 00:08:10.540
Inside that phrase lies the assumption that a person, too, is stock that can be stacked and stored — and nobody finds this strange anymore.

83
00:08:10.540 --> 00:08:18.350
This was Heidegger's darkest warning: in technology, the real danger is not the machines, but the way of seeing the machines teach us.

84
00:08:18.350 --> 00:08:23.280
Because that way of seeing makes us forget that any other way of seeing is possible.

85
00:08:23.280 --> 00:08:34.930
Around the same period, in nineteen sixty-six, the American psychologist Abraham Maslow put his finger on the same point in a far plainer sentence: if the only tool you have is a hammer,

86
00:08:34.930 --> 00:08:38.580
it's very tempting to treat everything you meet as a nail.

87
00:08:38.580 --> 00:08:43.990
This line is usually passed along as a joke, but there is a serious claim inside it.

88
00:08:43.990 --> 00:08:50.040
A tool doesn't merely solve the problem; it also determines what counts as a problem in the first place.

89
00:08:50.040 --> 00:08:53.210
The man holding a hammer sees nails in the world.

90
00:08:53.210 --> 00:08:58.460
The manager holding a spreadsheet sees everything unmeasurable as unimportant.

91
00:08:58.460 --> 00:09:03.390
An institution holding an algorithm doesn't see the person who won't turn into a number.

92
00:09:03.390 --> 00:09:11.360
In those same years the Canadian communication theorist Marshall McLuhan sharpened the matter to its finest edge: the medium is the message.

93
00:09:11.360 --> 00:09:15.490
On first hearing, the sentence sounds like wordplay.

94
00:09:15.490 --> 00:09:23.300
But what McLuhan meant was this: the content a medium carries is, most of the time, a bright lure that distracts us.

95
00:09:23.300 --> 00:09:28.550
The real effect comes from the way the medium itself changes the scale and rhythm of life.

96
00:09:28.550 --> 00:09:32.200
He takes the railway as his example.

97
00:09:32.200 --> 00:09:37.930
The railway did not bring humanity movement, or the wheel; both already existed.

98
00:09:37.930 --> 00:09:47.020
What the railway brought was entirely new cities, a new order of work, a new idea of leisure, clocks set to the same moment across a whole country.

99
00:09:47.020 --> 00:09:52.590
What was carried on the train is almost trivial next to this new world built by the manner of carrying.

100
00:09:52.590 --> 00:09:54.960
Translate that into today.

101
00:09:54.960 --> 00:10:00.050
What you watch on the screen is secondary to how the screen itself divides up your day.

102
00:10:00.050 --> 00:10:04.340
While we argue about the content, we have already accepted the form.

103
00:10:04.340 --> 00:10:10.390
The whole quarrel turns on what kind of nail it is; nobody questions that the hammer is in our hand.

104
00:10:10.390 --> 00:10:16.280
Eastern thought asked this question far earlier, and in a far calmer voice.

105
00:10:16.280 --> 00:10:26.170
In the book attributed to Zhuangzi, a philosopher who lived in China during the Warring States period — known in older sources as Chuang Tzu — there is a small scene.

106
00:10:26.170 --> 00:10:33.020
Zigong, one of Confucius's students, is returning from a long journey in the south when he passes by a garden.

107
00:10:33.020 --> 00:10:35.870
An old man is watering his plot.

108
00:10:35.870 --> 00:10:47.840
But he is watering it in a strange way: each time, he climbs down the steps into the well, fills a heavy jar by hand, hauls it up on his back, empties the water into the channel, then climbs down again.

109
00:10:47.840 --> 00:10:50.850
All that effort, so little water.

110
00:10:50.850 --> 00:10:53.860
Zigong can't hold back.

111
00:10:53.860 --> 00:10:59.430
Sir, he says, there is a device that can water a hundred fields in a single day.

112
00:10:59.430 --> 00:11:02.440
Little effort, much work done.

113
00:11:02.440 --> 00:11:04.170
Wouldn't you like one?

114
00:11:04.170 --> 00:11:14.220
What he's describing is a lever rigged at the wellhead, a weight at one end and a bucket at the other; light at the back, heavy at the front, carrying the water upward almost of its own accord.

115
00:11:14.220 --> 00:11:19.790
The old man straightens up, smiles, and says: I heard this from my master.

116
00:11:19.790 --> 00:11:24.080
A man who owns a machine comes to see his work as machine-like.

117
00:11:24.080 --> 00:11:29.170
A man who sees his work as machine-like grows a machine-like heart in his breast.

118
00:11:29.170 --> 00:11:37.940
And in a man who carries a machine heart in his breast, purity and wholeness break down; his spirit can find no rest, and he becomes restless.

119
00:11:37.940 --> 00:11:40.790
It isn't that I don't know about that contraption.

120
00:11:40.790 --> 00:11:44.440
I don't use it because I would be ashamed to use it.

121
00:11:44.440 --> 00:11:53.210
There is something harsh, almost unfair, about this story; but then, Zhuangzi's text has always been like that, written to provoke an argument.

122
00:11:53.210 --> 00:11:59.580
Yet the idea inside it is one of the most precise discoveries in the history of thought: the machine heart.

123
00:11:59.580 --> 00:12:02.750
The old man's objection isn't to the contraption.

124
00:12:02.750 --> 00:12:06.080
It's to the mark the contraption leaves on the breast.

125
00:12:06.080 --> 00:12:13.090
Once efficiency becomes a habit, a person begins to look not only at the field but at everything through the eyes of efficiency.

126
00:12:13.090 --> 00:12:23.780
A conversation turns into "a waste of time," a walk is judged "unproductive," a friendship gets weighed by "what it brings in." The tool leaves your hand; the measure stays in your head.

127
00:12:23.780 --> 00:12:29.990
But it would be a serious mistake to think that the East was against machines; that is reading only half the story.

128
00:12:29.990 --> 00:12:42.600
One of the most brilliant engineers of Islamic civilization, Ismail al-Jazari of Cizre, in the book he completed at the Artuqid court in twelve oh six, set down dozens of mechanisms — water clocks,

129
00:12:42.600 --> 00:12:53.130
automatic handwashing devices, self-moving musician figures, float-operated valves — with drawings, with measurements, explaining step by step how each one was to be built.

130
00:12:53.130 --> 00:12:56.780
A human being can take that much delight in machines.

131
00:12:56.780 --> 00:13:07.790
The same civilization could take Zhuangzi's question seriously and still love gears; because the question was never against the machine, it was about the direction the machine pulls the human being.

132
00:13:07.790 --> 00:13:17.520
Ibn Khaldun, in the Muqaddimah, completed in thirteen seventy-seven, approaches the matter from an entirely different place and says something that still startles us today.

133
00:13:17.520 --> 00:13:22.130
Crafts, he writes, settle into the human soul as a faculty.

134
00:13:22.130 --> 00:13:29.780
That is: a craft doesn't only leave calluses on the hands; it leaves a disposition in the mind, a point of view, a way of thinking.

135
00:13:29.780 --> 00:13:34.710
The reasoning of a man who knows carpentry carries the trace of carpentry.

136
00:13:34.710 --> 00:13:43.640
Ibn Khaldun describes this not as a loss but as a gain: a craft nourishes a person, multiplies his intelligence, gives him a new way of seeing.

137
00:13:43.640 --> 00:13:48.250
And immediately after, he sees the other face of it as well.

138
00:13:48.250 --> 00:13:59.740
Once affluence and comfort take hold, people's capacity to do their own work for themselves grows weak; a life that leans on the backs of others, or on the system, wears down one's resilience.

139
00:13:59.740 --> 00:14:06.590
This sits at the very center of Ibn Khaldun's analysis of civilization: comfort is a gain, but it isn't free.

140
00:14:06.590 --> 00:14:09.440
Put these three voices side by side.

141
00:14:09.440 --> 00:14:13.090
Zhuangzi's old man says the tool changes the heart.

142
00:14:13.090 --> 00:14:19.140
Al-Jazari shows that building the tool is, in itself, a pleasure and a form of knowledge.

143
00:14:19.140 --> 00:14:25.510
And Ibn Khaldun writes the sentence that unites the two: the tool leaves a faculty in the soul.

144
00:14:25.510 --> 00:14:30.280
What you use, even if it doesn't use you, leaves a disposition in you.

145
00:14:30.280 --> 00:14:38.570
So the question isn't "should I use the tool." The question is this: which faculty does this tool grow in me, and which one does it make unnecessary?

146
00:14:38.570 --> 00:14:42.380
Because it does both at once, and it only shows you one of them.

147
00:14:42.380 --> 00:14:44.750
Now look in your pocket.

148
00:14:44.750 --> 00:14:48.560
The thing sitting there is a tool without equal in human history.

149
00:14:48.560 --> 00:14:52.530
With it you can see the face of someone on the other side of the world.

150
00:14:52.530 --> 00:14:55.540
You don't get lost on a street you've never walked.

151
00:14:55.540 --> 00:15:00.150
Every question that crosses your mind finds an answer within seconds.

152
00:15:00.150 --> 00:15:10.840
These are not small things; to a person living a hundred years ago, every one of them would have counted as a miracle — and everything we complain about today is written on the bill for those miracles.

153
00:15:10.840 --> 00:15:12.890
Here is the bill.

154
00:15:12.890 --> 00:15:21.180
In a study published in twenty eleven by the American psychologist Betsy Sparrow and her colleagues, people were given a series of facts.

155
00:15:21.180 --> 00:15:26.910
One group was told the information would be saved on a computer and they could reach it whenever they liked.

156
00:15:26.910 --> 00:15:30.080
The other group was told the records would be deleted.

157
00:15:30.080 --> 00:15:36.290
The result was clear: those who knew the information would be stored were markedly worse at remembering it.

158
00:15:36.290 --> 00:15:41.700
But there was one thing they remembered very well — which folder the information was in.

159
00:15:41.700 --> 00:15:46.790
The mind, in other words, had memorized the address, not the content.

160
00:15:46.790 --> 00:15:54.440
Daniel Wegner, one of that study's co-authors, had proposed a concept for this years earlier: transactive memory.

161
00:15:54.440 --> 00:15:58.410
Human beings have always entrusted their memories to one another.

162
00:15:58.410 --> 00:16:07.020
In long marriages, one spouse remembers the dates and the other the names; in teams, who knows what is shared like a common map.

163
00:16:07.020 --> 00:16:09.870
Nobody calls that memory loss.

164
00:16:09.870 --> 00:16:15.440
But something new and different is happening here: the partner across from us is not a person.

165
00:16:15.440 --> 00:16:23.570
It doesn't tire, doesn't forget, has no limit — and most importantly, it doesn't ask us to remember anything in return.

166
00:16:23.570 --> 00:16:28.020
When a partnership becomes one-sided, it stops being a partnership.

167
00:16:28.020 --> 00:16:31.190
The same is true of our sense of direction.

168
00:16:31.190 --> 00:16:41.880
A study by the neuroscientist Eleanor Maguire, published in the year two thousand, showed that in the brains of London taxi drivers the rear portion of the hippocampus was larger than in a control group.

169
00:16:41.880 --> 00:16:52.890
These drivers had passed a punishing examination that required them to memorise tens of thousands of streets and connections, and those years of study had etched themselves into the physical structure of their brains.

170
00:16:52.890 --> 00:16:55.740
The city had grown inside their heads.

171
00:16:55.740 --> 00:17:05.470
Imaging studies carried out in later years revealed something else: when people follow turn-by-turn navigation, that region of the brain goes almost entirely quiet.

172
00:17:05.470 --> 00:17:10.560
If you are not building the city yourself, no city grows inside your head.

173
00:17:10.560 --> 00:17:14.530
And then there is the quietest clause of the contract.

174
00:17:14.530 --> 00:17:21.540
In the middle of the twentieth century, the American psychologist Burrhus Frederic Skinner discovered something in his laboratory:

175
00:17:21.540 --> 00:17:27.110
the reward that most powerfully reinforces a behaviour is not the one that arrives every time.

176
00:17:27.110 --> 00:17:31.560
It is the one that arrives sometimes, at moments you cannot predict.

177
00:17:31.560 --> 00:17:36.010
Uncertainty hardens a behaviour into something close to addiction.

178
00:17:36.010 --> 00:17:45.100
Every flick of your finger pulling a screen downwards today is the pull of a lever; sometimes nothing comes, and sometimes it is exactly what you were looking for.

179
00:17:45.100 --> 00:17:46.990
This is no accident.

180
00:17:46.990 --> 00:17:58.000
As the former design ethicist Tristan Harris has been explaining for years, these mechanisms were built not to capture our attention but to extract it — the way ore is torn out of a seam in mining.

181
00:17:58.000 --> 00:18:04.370
What Heidegger called "standing reserve" has finally come to take up residence in our waking hours.

182
00:18:04.370 --> 00:18:18.100
The American critic Neil Postman opens his book, written in nineteen ninety-two, with that very scene from ancient Egypt, and he says this: every technology is a Faustian bargain; it gives and it takes at the same time.

183
00:18:18.100 --> 00:18:23.830
So the right question is not "what will we gain" but "what are we losing in the very same moment".

184
00:18:23.830 --> 00:18:27.320
Yet Postman's real warning is subtler still.

185
00:18:27.320 --> 00:18:31.770
The bargain is not struck on equal terms for every part of society.

186
00:18:31.770 --> 00:18:37.020
The winner and the loser are, more often than not, not the same person.

187
00:18:37.020 --> 00:18:41.310
The picture that emerges from all of this is not a picture of catastrophe.

188
00:18:41.310 --> 00:18:47.360
Nobody is deceiving anybody, nobody is going to throw their phone in the bin, and nobody needs to.

189
00:18:47.360 --> 00:18:54.530
Thamus did not ban writing, Zhuangzi did not smash the well mechanism, Ibn Khaldun did not condemn the craft.

190
00:18:54.530 --> 00:18:58.020
Not one of them says "put the tool down".

191
00:18:58.020 --> 00:19:03.270
There is only one thing all of them are saying: do not sign the contract without reading it.

192
00:19:03.270 --> 00:19:06.440
But how does a person actually do that?

193
00:19:06.440 --> 00:19:08.810
Perhaps by a single measure.

194
00:19:08.810 --> 00:19:16.620
You cannot tell whether a tool has taken hold of you while you are using it, because in use the tool is invisible; it has melted into your hand.

195
00:19:16.620 --> 00:19:19.310
You can only tell once you set it down.

196
00:19:19.310 --> 00:19:26.160
What you feel on an afternoon when you have left your phone on the table, on a journey you set out on without navigation,

197
00:19:26.160 --> 00:19:31.570
on a page where you have had to build your own sentence yourself — that is what tells you the truth.

198
00:19:31.570 --> 00:19:35.700
The discomfort is normal; habit always resists.

199
00:19:35.700 --> 00:19:39.350
What matters is what comes after the discomfort.

200
00:19:39.350 --> 00:19:43.000
Does the faculty return after a while?

201
00:19:43.000 --> 00:19:46.330
Or is there a hollow where it used to be?

202
00:19:46.330 --> 00:19:52.860
Because the answer to the question — are you using the tool, or is it using you — is not in the tool.

203
00:19:52.860 --> 00:19:56.510
The answer is in what remains when you take your hand away.

204
00:19:56.510 --> 00:20:00.800
Nöron Philosophy'de bugünkü bölümün sonuna geldik.

205
00:20:00.800 --> 00:20:04.130
Yeni bir hikâyede yeniden buluşmak üzere.
